  "generated_text": "Given the quadratic equation \\(5x^2 + kx = 4\\), we know one root is 2. Let the other root be \\(r\\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\(x = 2\\) into the equation:\n\\[\n5(2)^2 + k(2) = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n20 + 2k = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n2k = -16\n\\]\n\\[\nk = -8\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(k = -8\\) back into the original equation:\n\\[\n5x^2 - 8x = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the fact that the product of the roots of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by \\(c/a\\). Here, \\(a = 5\\), \\(b = -8\\), and \\(c = 4\\).\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot r = \\frac{4}{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n2r = 4\n\\]\n\\[\nr = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the other root \\(r\\) is\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[2, '2']",
    "gold_answer": "Rearranging the given equation, we get $5x^2+kx-4=0$. That means that the product of the roots of the equation is $-4/5$. If one of the roots of the equation is 2, then the other must be $(-4/5)/2=\\boxed{-\\frac{2}{5}}$.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
